    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Elucidation of the molecular mechanisms that regulate synapse formation, maintenance, and homeostasis is required to understand neuron communication and how they contribute to the formation of neural networks and higher cognitive functions. Recent work has shown that C1q-like (C1QLs) are secreted proteins expressed in neurons in specific regions of the brain, and they act as ligands to the postsynaptic brain-specific angiogenesis inhibitor 3 (BAI3) adhesion GPCRs. Although these two proteins have been implicated in synapse development, fundamental questions regarding the calcium-binding properties of C1QL remain unanswered, and little is known about their downstream signaling with BAI3. As such, there is great merit in illuminating their integral roles in synapse regulation.\r\n\r\nThe globular domain of C1QL (gC1q) binds to thrombospondin repeat domains of BAI3 in a calcium-dependent manner. We generate mutants of calcium sites at the trimer interface of C1QL3. Using X-ray crystallography, we provide high-resolution structures of C1QL3 and its mutants to show changes in structure and ion capacity, which may be relevant for BAI3 receptor binding. We examine folding and stability using circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy and test calcium affinity using isothermal titration calorimetry (ITC) to narrow down the important calcium sites involved in BAI3 interactions. Together, the results suggest that C1QL and BAI3 mediate synapse homeostasis in the presence of calcium ions bound at specific sites. This research describes the calcium binding properties of the first known ligand to BAI3 and can be applied to the broader regulation of synapses in the brain through the interaction of these two proteins.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
